{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01To4dbNg74BsAify7HWXZLu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# MISSION — FORK-RUNS-FULL-SESSION-01 (a fork should be able to run a whole /leadv2 session, Phase 0→8)\n2\t\n3\tWork in `~/Projects/leadv2`. Founder-ordered, and he was emphatic about it: \"это надо сделать\n4\tточно.\"\n5\t\n6\t## Why this exists\n7\t\n8\tToday the lead is the only thing that can carry a task from intake to close. Everything else is a\n9\tworker that does one step and returns. That has two consequences the founder has now watched all\n10\tday:\n11\t\n12\t- The lead is a bottleneck. It spent a full day shepherding lanes through review rounds one at a\n13\t  time, and its own context is the scarce resource — every lane it supervises costs context that\n14\t  is then unavailable for the next one.\n15\t- When the lead's context fills, the session compacts, and continuity depends on files the lead\n16\t  remembered to write. A task in flight at that moment is at its most fragile.\n17\t\n18\tA fork inherits the parent's full context and runs independently. If a fork could run a complete\n19\t`/leadv2` session — Phase 0 intake through Phase 8 close — then a whole task, not a step, could be\n20\tdelegated.\n21\t\n22\t## What to determine and build\n23\t\n24\t**First, establish what actually blocks this today.** Do not assume it is impossible; do not\n25\tassume it is trivial. Walk `SKILL.md`'s phase list and the scripts each phase calls, and name\n26\tconcretely which ones cannot run in a fork and why — locking (`Phase 0` takes a lockfile and\n27\tregisters in `active.yaml`), worktree entry, the interactive gate at Phase 3, the deploy step at\n28\tPhase 6, the close writes at Phase 8. Some of these are genuine blockers and some are only\n29\tconventions.\n30\t\n31\t**Then build the smallest thing that works end to end.** A fork that can carry one real task from\n32\tintake to close is worth more than a design for one that could carry any task. Pick the narrowest\n33\ttask shape that exercises all nine phases and make that work. Say explicitly what shapes are NOT\n34\tsupported yet.\n35\t\n36\t**Get these three right or the feature is a liability:**\n37\t\n38\t- **Two sessions must not collide.** The lockfile and `active.yaml` registration exist because\n39\t  concurrent `/leadv2` runs corrupt each other. A fork running a full session is a second\n40\t  `/leadv2` by any other name. Say how you keep them apart.\n41\t- **Gate 1 in a fork.** The one human gate cannot silently auto-accept just because the parent is\n42\t  elsewhere. `LEADV2_ASYNC_QUESTIONS` and `leadv2-ask.sh` already exist for exactly this — use\n43\t  that path, or say why it does not fit.\n44\t- **A fork that dies must be visible.** Today a background worker that dies silently looks\n45\t  identical to one still working; that has cost real work repeatedly. A fork owning a whole task\n46\t  makes that failure much more expensive, so it needs a liveness signal the parent can check.\n47\t\n48\t## Hard constraints\n49\t- No phase may be skipped to make this work. A fork that runs Phase 0→8 runs all of it, including\n50\t  review and live verification. A \"fork session\" that quietly omits the gates is worse than no\n51\t  fork at all.\n52\t- Plugin repo only.\n53\t\n54\t## Evidence required\n55\tA transcript or log of one real task carried by a fork from intake to a written\n56\t`phase8-passed.flag`, with the review gate's verdict visible in it. Report to\n57\t`docs/missions/FORK-RUNS-FULL-SESSION-01.report.md`. End with DELIVERABLE_COMPLETE.\n58\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1c3902b-a0e6-436a-8cd5-09ce428ca8c7","uuid":"0970d786-98e7-4146-bdb2-09cf443da4e2","timestamp":"2026-08-15T04:06:35.791Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kostiantyn.vlasenko/Projects/leadv2/docs/missions/FORK-RUNS-FULL-SESSION-01.md","content":"# MISSION — FORK-RUNS-FULL-SESSION-01 (a fork should be able to run a whole /leadv2 session, Phase 0→8)\n\nWork in `~/Projects/leadv2`. Founder-ordered, and he was emphatic about it: \"это надо сделать\nточно.\"\n\n## Why this exists\n\nToday the lead is the only thing that can carry a task from intake to close. Everything else is a\nworker that does one step and returns. That has two consequences the founder has now watched all\nday:\n\n- The lead is a bottleneck. It spent a full day shepherding lanes through review rounds one at a\n  time, and its own context is the scarce resource — every lane it supervises costs context that\n  is then unavailable for the next one.\n- When the lead's context fills, the session compacts, and continuity depends on files the lead\n  remembered to write. A task in flight at that moment is at its most fragile.\n\nA fork inherits the parent's full context and runs independently. If a fork could run a complete\n`/leadv2` session — Phase 0 intake through Phase 8 close — then a whole task, not a step, could be\ndelegated.\n\n## What to determine and build\n\n**First, establish what actually blocks this today.** Do not assume it is impossible; do not\nassume it is trivial. Walk `SKILL.md`'s phase list and the scripts each phase calls, and name\nconcretely which ones cannot run in a fork and why — locking (`Phase 0` takes a lockfile and\nregisters in `active.yaml`), worktree entry, the interactive gate at Phase 3, the deploy step at\nPhase 6, the close writes at Phase 8. Some of these are genuine blockers and some are only\nconventions.\n\n**Then build the smallest thing that works end to end.** A fork that can carry one real task from\nintake to close is worth more than a design for one that could carry any task. Pick the narrowest\ntask shape that exercises all nine phases and make that work. Say explicitly what shapes are NOT\nsupported yet.\n\n**Get these three right or the feature is a liability:**\n\n- **Two sessions must not collide.** The lockfile and `active.yaml` registration exist because\n  concurrent `/leadv2` runs corrupt each other. A fork running a full session is a second\n  `/leadv2` by any other name. Say how you keep them apart.\n- **Gate 1 in a fork.** The one human gate cannot silently auto-accept just because the parent is\n  elsewhere. `LEADV2_ASYNC_QUESTIONS` and `leadv2-ask.sh` already exist for exactly this — use\n  that path, or say why it does not fit.\n- **A fork that dies must be visible.** Today a background worker that dies silently looks\n  identical to one still working; that has cost real work repeatedly. A fork owning a whole task\n  makes that failure much more expensive, so it needs a liveness signal the parent can check.\n\n## Hard constraints\n- No phase may be skipped to make this work. A fork that runs Phase 0→8 runs all of it, including\n  review and live verification. A \"fork session\" that quietly omits the gates is worse than no\n  fork at all.\n- Plugin repo only.\n\n## Evidence required\nA transcript or log of one real task carried by a fork from intake to a written\n`phase8-passed.flag`, with the review gate's verdict visible in it. Report to\n`docs/missions/FORK-RUNS-FULL-SESSION-01.report.md`. End with DELIVERABLE_COMPLETE.\n","numLines":58,"startLine":1,"totalLines":58}}}
